# {{.Target}}

A FastAPI application for inbound Twilio ConversationRelay calls. Change the
source package and run `unmute compile` to rebuild it.

Twilio handles speech. This app keeps the conversation, runs your tools, and
streams replies as text. You choose where to host it. Start with the
[Twilio setup guide](https://unmute.ai/telephony/twilio-conversation-relay)
if you need an account, a number, or your first deployment.
{{if .Router}}
This build uses **SLNG Context Router** before the upstream LLM. It reuses
answers it judges cacheable and calls the upstream for other turns. Set
`{{.ModelKeyEnv}}` and the upstream credentials listed in `.env.example`.
The upstream credentials are sent to SLNG on each model request. Change the
source binding's `agent_id` when prompt changes should invalidate cached
answers. SLNG hosts the router; you still host this app.
{{end}}
## Run it

Compile creates this project locally. To publish it, upload its contents
(excluding `.env`) to a Python host, connect a deployment repository to a
container host, or push a built image to your host's registry. The
[hosting walkthrough](https://unmute.ai/telephony/twilio-conversation-relay#4-host-the-application)
includes a Render example and a VM alternative. Running the commands below on
your laptop starts only a local app; a tunnel is needed for Twilio to reach it.

1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in the values. Keep secrets out of Git.
   Set `{{.Env.PublicURL}}` to the exact public HTTPS origin, with no path.
{{- if ne .Region "us1"}}
   This package's calls are handled in the Twilio Region `{{.Region}}`
   ([Twilio Regions]({{.Docs.Regions}})). `{{.Env.AuthToken}}` must be that
   region's Auth Token, not the US1 one: Twilio signs the region's requests
   with it ([regional credentials]({{.Docs.RegionalTokens}})). The number's
   routing region must also be `{{.Region}}`
   ([inbound processing region]({{.Docs.InboundRouting}})).
{{- end}}
2. From this folder, run Python {{.Python}} on your host:

   ```sh
   uv run --env-file .env python app.py
   ```

   Or build and run the included image:

   ```sh
   docker build -t {{.Project}} .
   docker run --env-file .env -e PORT=8080 -p 8080:8080 --stop-timeout 35 {{.Project}}
   ```

3. Configure your host's TLS endpoint to forward HTTP and WebSockets to the
   app. Check `GET /healthz` through that public endpoint.
4. From the source package directory, connect your number:

   ```sh
   unmute deploy --target {{.Target}} --dry-run
   unmute deploy --target {{.Target}}
   ```

   Deploy reads the Twilio values from your shell or the source package's
   `.env`, not this build folder's `.env`. Supply the same account, token and
   public origin, plus the number SID listed below. The command sets the
   number's webhook to `https://<host>/voice` with HTTP POST. The app generates
   the `wss://<host>/conversation` URL from `{{.Env.PublicURL}}`; do not put
   the WebSocket URL in the number's webhook field.
5. Call the number. Check the greeting, a reply, interruption, and `end_call`.
   Read the app's log and Twilio's call inspector if a step fails.

### Hosting requirements

Twilio requires a reachable `wss://` server
([ConversationRelay]({{.Docs.ConversationRelay}})); it does not host this app.
Use any container or Python host that provides:

- A public HTTPS origin with TLS and long-lived WebSocket connections.
- Access to `/voice`, `/conversation`, and `/connect-action` on that origin.
- Environment values from `.env.example`, supplied as secrets.
- One process and one instance. Call state and capacity are held in memory.
- Port forwarding to `0.0.0.0:$PORT` (default 8080).
- A `/healthz` health check and about 35 seconds from SIGTERM to SIGKILL.
- Availability when calls arrive; a sleeping service can miss a call.

On SIGTERM the app stops accepting calls, allows running calls up to 20 seconds,
and then ends them. `/healthz` reports 503 while draining or when abandoned
handlers occupy every slot. Otherwise it reports 200 with `artifact_id`;
a healthy process can still be at call capacity.

For a laptop trial, start `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:8080`, set
`{{.Env.PublicURL}}` to its HTTPS origin, then start the app. If the origin
changes, update it, restart the app, and route the number again. There is no
`unmute dev` loop for this target.

## Connect the number

`unmute deploy` with `--target {{.Target}}` configures an existing number's
voice webhook. It does not upload this app, buy a number, or place a call.

| Environment name | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `{{.Env.AccountSID}}` | Account that owns the number; also required by the app. |
| `{{.Env.AuthToken}}` | Account Auth Token; must match the app. |
| `{{.Env.PublicURL}}` | Public HTTPS origin; must match the app. |
{{- range .DeployEnv}}
| `{{.}}` | Number SID, `PN` followed by 32 characters; deploy only. |
{{- end}}

Deploy checks the number's voice capability, routing region, and conflicting
TwiML App, SIP trunk, or fallback settings. It verifies the hosted build's
`artifact_id` and signed `/voice` response. A dry run stops before writing.
A normal run saves the previous route in a private rollback file, updates only
`VoiceUrl` and `VoiceMethod`, and checks them again. Inspect the Console if a
write's outcome is `unknown`; do not assume it failed. Run one deploy at a time
per number. The model keys are needed by the app, not by deploy.

For manual setup ([Twilio onboarding]({{.Docs.Onboarding}})):
{{range .ManualSteps}}
- {{.}}
{{- end}}

A call reaches `POST /voice`, which returns generated TwiML pointing to
`wss://<host>/conversation`. Twilio opens the signed WebSocket and exchanges
text with the app. `/connect-action` returns hangup when the session ends,
{{- if .Logic}} unless your `next_twiml()` supplies another action.
{{- else}} after `end_call`.
{{- end}}
Only a real call verifies the WebSocket, speech, model, and hangup together.

### Update or restore

Edit the source package, compile, upload the new build to your chosen host,
and run `unmute deploy --target {{.Target}}` again. Deploy refuses a host
running a different build. Updating the app can end active calls during drain.

Compile replaces this folder, preserving only `.env`. Keep host-specific files
in the source package's `hosting/{{.Target}}/` folder; compile copies them here.

To restore the old number route, open the private snapshot printed by deploy.
First check the number still uses its `new_voice_url` with POST and has no
TwiML App, trunk, or fallback URL. If it differs, resolve the intervening
change before restoring. Otherwise set its webhook back to the snapshot's
`voice_url` and `voice_method`. This restores routing, not the hosted build.

## Runtime behavior

- **Security:** the app validates `X-Twilio-Signature` for HTTP requests and
  the WebSocket handshake against the configured public origin
  ([webhook security]({{.Docs.Security}})).
- **Speech:** Twilio handles listening and speaking. DTMF detection is off.
- **Streaming:** replies arrive incrementally, with `last: true` on the final
  fragment. That marks text completion, not playback completion
  ([WebSocket messages]({{.Docs.Messages}})).
- **Interruptions:** the app stops the reply and retains the text reported as
  heard. It cannot recall tokens already sent to Twilio.
- **Capacity:** at most {{.MaxSessions}} calls. A full app returns `<Hangup/>`.
- **Tools:** arguments and results are validated. Handlers run to completion;
  after {{.ToolDeadline}} seconds the model receives an unknown outcome.
  Bound tool network timeouts: a running handler retains its call slot.
  There are at most {{.MaxRounds}} tool rounds per turn.
- **Hangup:** `end_call` ends the session. A goodbye in the same turn may be cut
  off. The app sends `end` and lets Twilio close the WebSocket, waiting up to
  five seconds before closing it itself.
- **Failures:** model failures use a fixed spoken response; three consecutive
  failures end the call. Logs contain event names, call/session IDs and error
  classes, not transcripts, prompts, tool arguments, or secrets.
{{if .Logic}}
## Custom logic

This build calls `logic/respond(session)` for each caller turn. Edit
`{{.Logic.Path}}` in the source package. The app manages signatures,
interruptions, capacity, and shutdown.

`session.end(reason, **data)` ends the session after the reply. An optional
`next_twiml(handoff)` in `logic/__init__.py` returns TwiML for the next action.
`handoff.resume(note, greeting="...")` resumes the saved conversation within
10 minutes, in this process only. `session.twilio` is the account's Twilio REST
client. See the [custom logic reference](https://unmute.ai/targets/twilio#bring-your-own-agent-logic)
for the complete interface.
{{end}}
